hi I'm Rachel and I'm a tasty producer  today I'm gonna share with you some of  my favorite tips and tricks for finding  vegan options when I go out to eat one  cuisine that has a lot of vegan options  is Chinese food look for veggie  dumplings eggplant with garlic sauce  tofu with mixed vegetables rice and  veggie spring rolls instead of egg rolls  you'll want to watch out for eggs and  fried Rice's or in noodles like lo mein  and fish or oyster sauce used in sauces  or soups  I love ordering Chinese because there  are so many sauces and flavor profiles  to try which can make tofu and  vegetables feel like a totally different  dish every time another great option is  middle-eastern food my go-to is here our  hummus falafel Israeli salad baba  ghanoush tabbouleh and pita bread you'll  want to look out for yogurt which is  used in two Zeki sauce and feta which is  commonly used in salads one tip is to  substitute tahini which is made from  sesame seeds for two Zeki to drizzle on  falafel wraps and platters  I love middle-eastern food because it's  a great place to take a big group of  friends where there will be something  for everyone one of my favorite cuisines  for vegan options is Indian food some  great choices are eggplant with potatoes  chana masala rice dosas filled with  potatoes or lentils non-dairy sauces and  chutneys papadum and samosas and pakoras  here you'll want to avoid ghee which is  a clarified butter and typically used to  make naan bread cream or yogurt which  can be found in curries and sauces and  paneer which is a type of cheese some  tips here are to substitute papadum for  naan bread and if there's a dish you  want to try that contains cream or  yogurt ask your server if it's possible  to substitute coconut milk and they  might be able to accommodate you I love  Indian food because a lot of the dishes  are inherently vegetarian or vegan so  there are tons of options to choose from  another cuisine that is usually a safe  bet is Mexican food here I like to get  rice and beans salsa guac and tortilla  chips and veggie burritos or tacos just  be sure to check that the rice and beans  weren't cooked in  or beef broth and make sure to omit any  cheese or sour cream from any of your  dishes one tip is to substitute  guacamole for cheese and sour cream on a  burrito to replace that creaminess even  if you have to make a few substitutions  it's usually pretty easy to find vegan  options at a Mexican restaurant and  personally as long as I have guac I'm a  happy camper next up is Thai food I'll  usually get pad CU with tofu and veggies  Thai red curry papaya salad and  sometimes if they have it a vegan  coconut broth based soup you have to be  careful with Thai food because a lot of  dishes do contain fish sauce oyster  sauce and shrimp paste so ask your  server if those can be omitted  while rice noodles don't contain egg egg  is very common in pad Cu and pad thai so  make sure to ask to have it removed  while fish sauce is prevalent in Thai  food there are restaurants that don't  include it so I would just call the  restaurant ahead of time to be safe so  Italian food is when things start to get  a little trickier but I'm gonna show you  how you can still make it work I'll  usually get a veggie pizza without the  cheese pasta with marinara sauce  bruschetta and mixed olives your biggest  obstacles here will be cheese which is  in basically everything egg which may be  in some fresh pastas and you just want  to make sure that the tomato sauce  wasn't cooked with any meat but that  being said Italian food can be  surprisingly vegan friendly and don't  knock pizza without cheese until you try  it last up we have our most difficult  category the steak house here side  dishes are your friends look for sides  of roasted or steamed veggies rice pilaf  or baked potatoes the biggest ingredient  you'll want to avoid here well besides  the steak is butter so ask if the sides  are cooked in it and if you're able to  substitute oil so you're not just eating  a sad baked potato another option is to  ask your server if the kitchen can  prepare you a vegan friendly dish using  ingredients from the menu like pasta and  vegetables and if worst comes to worst  remember there's always fries and salad  while eating vegan at a steak house  isn't the ideal situation with a little  creative  you can still enjoy a tasty meal so the  dishes I mentioned tend to be vegan  friendly but keep in mind that every  restaurant is different  so always double-check with your server  to see how a dish is prepared and if any  of this felt too restrictive to you just  do the best that you can the important  thing to remember is that when you ask  for vegan options it lets restaurants  know there's a demand for them and  they'll start including them in their  menus more and more finding being in  options when you go out to eat might  seem difficult at first but with a  little practice it's totally doable  whether it's substituting an ingredient  or getting creative with the menu  there's almost always a vegan option out  there  [Music]  you  [Applause]
 

